        {
          "id": "ch14_ex10_q1i",
          "number": "1(i)",
          "category": null,
          "questionText": "If $\\sin x = 0.3$, $\\cos\\alpha = 0.6$ and $\\tan\\theta = 0.7$, find the value of $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right)$.",
          "questionFigures": [],
          "solution": {
            "formula": "$\\sin(\\pi + u) = -\\sin u$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right) = \\cos\\alpha$.",
            "steps": [
              "Given: $\\cos\\alpha = 0.6$. Goal: $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right)$.",
              "Write $\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha = \\pi + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right)$.",
              "$\\sin\\left(\\pi + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right)\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right) = -\\cos\\alpha$.",
              "$= -0.6$.",
              "Check with $\\alpha = 0$: $\\sin\\frac{3\\pi}{2} = -1 = -\\cos 0$. Correct."
            ],
            "finalAnswer": "$\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right) = -0.6$",
            "solutionFigures": [],
            "tip": "Break $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ into $\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and apply one known property at a time."
          }
        },
        {
